  "worked_solution": "The denominator is $1+\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $1-\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $1-\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\text{i}}{1+\\text{i}} & =\\frac{3\\text{i}}{1+\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1-\\text{i}}{1-\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\text{i}-3{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\text{i}-3(-1)}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\text{i}+3}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\text{i}+3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\text{i}+3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
